  "steps": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "claim": "For sound in a stationary medium the observed frequency is f' = f·(v + v_D)/(v – v_S), where v is the speed of sound, v_D is the detector velocity component toward the source (positive toward the source) and v_S is the source velocity component toward the detector (positive toward the detector).",
      "evidence": "Standard Doppler‑shift formula for acoustic waves in a medium (e.g., physics textbooks).",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "No Doppler shift (f' = f) occurs when the numerator equals the denominator, giving v + v_D = v – v_S, which simplifies to v_D = –v_S.",
      "evidence": "Algebraic manipulation of the formula in step 1; setting f' = f and cancelling the common factor f.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Because v_D is defined positive toward the source and v_S positive toward the detector, the relation v_D = –v_S means the detector moves opposite to the source with the same speed magnitude; equivalently, the two objects can move in the same direction with equal speeds relative to the medium.",
      "evidence": "If both move to the right, the source’s motion toward the detector is positive (v_S > 0) while the detector’s motion toward the source is negative (v_D < 0); the equality v_D = –v_S is satisfied when |v_D| = |v_S|.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "In option (C) the source and detector travel in the same direction; they can choose equal speeds, making |v_D| = |v_S| and thus satisfying v_D = –v_S, so no Doppler shift is possible.",
      "evidence": "Interpretation of option (C) allows the special case of equal speeds; with that choice the condition from step 3 holds.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "In options (A), (B) and (D) the relative motion along the line of sight cannot satisfy v_D = –v_S unless both velocities are zero, which contradicts the wording that at least one object moves.",
      "evidence": "Applying the sign definitions: (A) v_S > 0, v_D = 0 → v_D ≠ –v_S; (B) v_D > 0, v_S = 0 → v_D ≠ –v_S; (D) source and detector move away, giving v_S > 0 and v_D < 0, but magnitudes differ, so equality fails.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [2]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Therefore the only situation among the choices that can produce zero Doppler shift is option (C).",
      "evidence": "Logical combination of steps 4 and 5.",
      "weight": 0.07,
      "depends_on": [4,5]
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "The answer is C.",
      "evidence": "Conclusion from step 6.",
      "weight": 0.03,
      "depends_on": [6]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
  "confidence": 0.94,
  "considered": [
    {
      "answer": "A",
      "reason_rejected": "A requires a moving source toward a stationary detector, which inevitably changes the source‑detector distance and produces a Doppler shift."
    },
    {
      "answer": "B",
      "reason_rejected": "B requires a moving detector toward a stationary source, also changing the distance and causing a shift."
    },
    {
      "answer": "D",
      "reason_rejected": "D has the source and detector moving apart, guaranteeing a non‑zero relative velocity and a shift."
    }
  ]
}
